OK, sweet! I pushed up a few minor tweaks of my own, almost all editorial. This now looks good to me.

@jakearchibald, I think we addressed your comments, so I will dismiss your review, but if you have the time, a re-review would be much appreciated.

From what I recall, this had pretty active and helpful support from WebKit at least in https://github.com/whatwg/html/issues/2806. @smfr and/or @othermaciej, would you like to take a look at the results, probably using the diff links in the OP to see the rendered additions? From what I can see we haven't yet addressed the WebKit request to have a feature for a placeholder image while it is loading ("lowsrc"), but I think we went through a lot of the other questions in that thread and resolved them; please correct me if I'm wrong.

@bengreenstein, are there web platform tests for this yet?

@BitpopCoin You should able to listen for the load event on the image tag.

```html
<img src="#" id="notify" lazyload="on" />
```
```javascript
const img = document.querySelector('#notify');

img.addEventListener('load', e => {
  console.log('The image has been loaded!')
})
```

Which is actually very cool.

@othermaciej 

Regarding placeholders, your https://github.com/whatwg/html/issues/2806#issuecomment-379996290 sparked this issue: https://github.com/whatwg/html/issues/3631, I take it as you have missed that as there were _some_ feedback.

^^

@othermaciej 

Also, regarding the naming of this feature, others expressed their opinions here:
https://github.com/w3c/webappsec-feature-policy/issues/193#issuecomment-460109176

@bengreenstein If we did that, we'd also want to provide an alternate way of setting it, since `<picture>` is not required to contain an `<img>`. But I'm also not sure it is a super good idea. Ideally, a `<source>` for a `<picture>` should be able to start loading as soon as it's parsed if it is an eager load. This will generally be earlier than the nested `<img>` is parsed. So by the time the browser is told to do lazy loading, it's too late and it may have started the load already.

In addition, browsers that do preload scanning (i.e. a quick scan to start loading sub resources even before the parser has reached them, such as due to a blocking script) also won't see the `<img>` before the `<source>`.

For these reasons, I don't think the suggestion will actually work, despite being syntactically neat. Or at least, to make it would would require never starting the load for a `<source>` until the close tag of `<picture>` is hit, which would potentially be a performance hit.

>WebKit's preload scanner does not wait until it sees an `img` inside `picture` before preloading a source

@othermaciej so it will preload even if there's no `img` tag for displaying?
Using `picture` does seem like a natural way to load a lighter `placeholder` image.

@annevk 

> I don't understand what primitive "Responses that begin after byte 0 must be ignored." builds on.

Yeah, I agree that's pretty hand-wavy. Here's how I did it in background fetch https://wicg.github.io/background-fetch/#extract-content-range-values-algorithm. Happy for that to be moved into fetch if it's something multiple specs need to do.

In general, I'm still worried about the vagueness of the auto behaviour.

```js
const img = new Image();
img.src = url;
img.onload = () => {
  // ready!
};
```

Or more recently:

```js
const img = new Image();
img.src = url;
await img.decode();
// ready!
```

…are common ways to preload images. It's also likely that developers will wait for these to load before activating particular behaviours (especially if the images are going to be used in a canvas).

Unless I'm misreading, this spec change means `onload` or `onerror` may never fire, and `img.decode()` may never resolve.

It feels like when `img.decode` is called, the image must be fetched. But I'm less sure what to do about the first example. Thoughts:

When `src` is set, queue a (micro)task. If the image is not connected to a document, load the image eagerly. (I'm not sure this will solve all preloading cases)

Or, if the `img` was not parser-inserted, and `loading` is auto, it must eagerly load.

Or, load eagerly if a 'load' listener is added (urgh).

> > When `src` is set, queue a (micro)task. If the image is not connected to a document, load the image eagerly. (I'm not sure this will solve all preloading cases)
> 
> Sounds right to me (unless the author explicitly adds "loading=lazy" to their out-of-document image).

It's worth a try I guess. Although this would break:

```js
const div = document.createElement('div');
div.style.display = 'none';
document.body.append(div);

const img = new Image();
img.src = url;
div.append(img);

img.onload = () => {
  div.style.display = 'block';
};
```

I don't know how popular this pattern is in the wild.

@addyosmani @kinu @jakearchibald @domfarolino 

I was also pretty excited about the range request mechanism – both for reserving space on layouts, and also as a way forward for possible native low-quality image placeholders, when paired with a format that could actually get a paintable frame into the first couple of kilobytes.

In my testing this was surprisingly difficult with Progressive JPEG, though. Perhaps some faraway day if/when a super-progressive format like [FUIF](https://css-tricks.com/fuif-responsive-images-by-design/) or [JPEG-XL](https://jpeg.org/jpegxl/index.html) gains adoption, we can revisit this, maybe with a new `loading` attribute value.

@kinu you wrote

> To be clear; I think Chrome's current metadata-fetching behavior for automatic LazyLoad for Data-Saving users is just fine and makes sense. But just wasn't sure if it's something that should be included as an optional implicit behavior of this API.

It's not entirely clear to me what this means. Will Chrome continue to issue range requests? Also, I thought the way it was included here was as a requirement?
